You can restrict what an agent can do using the `permission` config. This lets you control which tools the agent can use, and set rules for what commands it can run or which files it can access.

For example, to restrict bash commands and file edits:
```json
{
  "permission": {
    "bash": {
      "*": "ask",
      "git *": "allow",
      "npm *": "allow"
    },
    "edit": {
      "*": "deny",
      "./src/**": "allow"
    }
  }
}
```

You can also use `external_directory` to control which paths outside the working directory are accessible.

For macOS sandboxing specifically, OpenCode doesn't use macOS Seatbelt profiles — the permission system is the equivalent mechanism. Set `"bash": "ask"` to require approval for every shell command.

Docs: https://opencode.ai/docs/permissions/

To use a local Ollama model in OpenCode Desktop, you need to configure Ollama as a local provider in your config. The "Ollama Cloud" you see is the default hosted option — you need to add a local Ollama provider manually.

Add this to your `opencode.json` (or `~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc`):

```json
{
  "provider": {
    "ollama": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "options": { "baseURL": "http://localhost:11434/v1" },
      "models": {
        "llama3.2-vision": { "name": "llama3.2-vision" }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Then run `/models` in the TUI to refresh the model list, and you should see your local Ollama models.

For more details, see the docs:
https://opencode.ai/docs/providers/

LSP diagnostics are fed to the AI assistant to help it understand your code — but if a particular LSP server is producing noisy or incorrect diagnostics (like missing headers or unknown types for embedded development), you can configure or disable it.

**To disable LSP entirely:**
```json
{ "lsp": false }
```

**To disable a specific LSP server** (e.g. clangd which is likely causing your C errors):
```json
{
  "lsp": {
    "clangd": { "disabled": true }
  }
}
```

**To keep LSP enabled but configure it**, you can pass initialization options or environment variables. For clangd specifically, you might want to configure your compile commands (`compile_commands.json`) to fix the missing header errors at the source.

For full details:
https://opencode.ai/docs/lsp/
